In earlier times, it was called 'Tajima Niimasu Kaminoyashiro'. It is the only [[Myojin Taisha]] in [[Hizen Province]], and was previously classified as [[Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Kokuhei Chusha]] due to renovations during the [[Meiji period]]. Currently, it is a [[beppyo shrine]] of the [[Association of Shinto Shrines]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=別表神社とは？御朱印めぐりに参考になる「別表神社一覧」とマップ {{!|draft}} 開運戦隊ゴシュインジャー |url=https://jinja-gosyuin.com/beppyou/ |access-date=2023-04-02 |website=jinja-gosyuin.com|draft}}</ref> It is associated with [[Matsura Sayohime]], whose poem was written by [[Yamanoue no Okura]], and is said to be the original shrine (roots) of [[Munakata Taisha]], so it is sometimes called Moto-Munakata

It is a Munakata shrine<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kalland |first=Arne |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wp4xhRXsARcC&dq=Tashima+Shrine&pg=PA47 |title=Fishing Villages in Tokugawa, Japan |date=1995-01-01 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-1632-2 |language=en|draft}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Rots |first1=Aike P. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pcjZDwAAQBAJ&dq=Tashima+Shrine&pg=PT104 |title=Sacred Heritage in Japan |last2=Teeuwen |first2=Mark |date=2020-04-02 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-000-04563-5 |language=en|draft}}</ref>

