==Content from Matsura Sayohime==
{{Short description|Legendary heroine in Japanese mythology}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=November 2016}}
[[File:Statue of Princess Sayo.jpg|thumb|300px|Bronze statue of Sayohime, near the summit of Kagamiyama]]<!--Though some may argue against this sculpture as top image, she is likely represented in period costume and hairstyle, whereas the ukiyoe has her wearing Edo period women clothes and hairdo, so moved the Kuniyoshi print below.-->

{{nihongo|'''Matsura Sayohime''' or '''Matsura no Sayohime'''|松浦佐用姫|extra="'''Lady Sayo of Matsura'''"}} ('Lady Sayo of Matsura'<ref name=manyoshu-shinkokai/>{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|Also ''''Lady Matsura''''<ref name=japan-office1915/>}}) or '''Matsuura Sayohime'''{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|Although the reading is not always clear, some late (Edo Period) primary sources appear to style her "Matsuura", and in the paper by Yanagita (1971) ({{harvp|Yanagita|1942}} [1927]) her name is read "Matsuura Sayohime" translated ''''Princess Matsuura'''' (English citation by Okuyama.<ref name=okuyama/>}} was a legendary heroine in [[Japanese mythology]], the wife of the historical [[Ōtomo no Satehiko]]. She is referred  to as '''Lady Otohi''' or '''Otohihime''' in an alternate ancient source.

The core legend was that she climbed atop a hill and so piteously waved her scarf (''hire'') at her husband's departing warship that the location afterwards was remembered as Hire-furi-no-mine or "Scarf-Waving Peak", now known as {{illm|Mount Kagami (Saga)|ja|鏡山 (佐賀県)|lt=Mount Kagami}} in the confines of the present-day city of [[Karatsu, Saga]]. The locale fell within the former [[Matsura-gun]], referred to as the "Matsuura region" in modern parlance.

However, the variant legend added that she was afterwards  visited by her husband's look-alike and though she discovered the imposter to be a snake, she had gone missing and was eventually found dead. Later ''[[otogizōshi]]'' (fairy tale) versions of ''Sayohime'', which were also readapted as {{illm|sekkyō-bushi|ja|説経節|lt=''sekkyō-bushi''}}, i.e., Buddhist "sermon ballad" pieces under the title '''''Matsura chōja''''', contained an alteration of this plot where the heroine, in an act of [[filial piety]], selling herself to be sacrificed to a serpent deity. Her life is ultimately spared in the fairy tale version.

The legend also recalled that she dropped a precious mirror which was a gift from her husband, and later it came to be believed that she had committed suicide by throwing herself into the river while clutching the mirror. This was then dramatized for the [[noh]] theatre in the early [[15th century]].

The motif of Otohihime/Sayohime waving her scarf from the mountaintop has been illustrated in picture books and woodblock prints.

== Old legends ==
The legends of Matsura Sayohime (aka Otohihime) waving her scarf (''hire'', a piece of long cloth worn as part of the attire) at the warship carrying her husband are preserved in two ancient pieces of writing, written down some two hundred years after the supposed event took place in the 6th century.{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|pp=11–12}}<ref name="Cranston"/>

=== Manyoshu version ===
Matsura Sayohime, according to the oldest account{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|p=21}} related in the poem collection ''[[Man'yōshū]]'' (8th century), was the  wife of Ōtomo no Satehiko (or Sadehiko). In order to bid farewell to her husband, who was on a ship bound for Korea for a military campaign, she climbed atop a certain mountain peak.<ref name=manyoshu-shinkokai/> So deep was her sorrow: she seemed "gut-wrenchedly" chagrined and darkened-hearted as if her "soul had vanished".<ref name="inaoka" />{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|The verbatim original text reads that she "disappointedly cleft her liver and despondently erased her soul{{linktext|悵然}}に肝を断ち、{{linktext|黯然}}に魂を{{linktext|銷}}す". Since this cannot be taken literally, the paraphrase in modern language (Inaoka) was consulted.}} Finally, she managed to remove her own scarf and wave it, drawing others around her inevitably to tears.<ref name="Cranston" />{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|p=21}}{{sfnp|Nagano|1974|p=1}}

==== Date ====
Ōtomo no Satehiko was known historically to have been dispatched on two military expeditions to Korea, and the setting of the legend concerns his first trip which occurred in the year 537;<ref name="Cranston" /> though it is the other ''Fudoki'' version of the legend described below which supplies the detail which leads to narrowing down this date.{{Refn|Satehiko's embarked on his military assignments to Korea in the years 537 and 562, and the first trip was to aid the Japanese-aligned [[Mimana]] (''[[Nihon shoki]]'').<ref>{{harvp|Aston (tr.)|1896}}, ''Nihongi'' '''II''': [https://books.google.com/books?id=tDFBAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA35 35], 86.</ref> Note that  "Mimana" is mentioned in the ''Hizen fudoki'' version of the legend.<ref name="Cranston"/>}}

==== Location ====
The [[Matsura-gun|Matsura region]], Sayohime's alluded place of origin, spans the current-day Nagasaki and [[Saga Prefecture]]s.{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|p=60}} However, the specific mountaintop that had been dubbed {{nihongo|Hirefurinomine|領巾麾嶺|extra="Scarf-Waving Peak"}}, as attested in the ''Man'yōshū'',<ref name="Cranston" />{{sfnp|Nagano|1974|p=1}} has been identified as the summit of {{illm|Mount Kagami (Saga)|ja|鏡山 (佐賀県)|lt=Mount Kagami}}, on the eastern edge of the city of [[Karatsu, Saga]].<ref name="Cranston" /><ref name="daijiten-hirefurinomine" />{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|Japanese sources state the mountain overlooks Karatsu Bay ({{illm|Karatsu wan|ja|唐津湾}}),<ref name=daijiten-hirefurinomine/> whereas Cranston says there is a view to "Matsura Bay".<ref name="Cranston"/> It is not entirely clear whether he meant {{nihongo|Matsura-gata|松浦潟}} on the current map (a "lagoon" which is part of Karatsu Bay), or the old name {{nihongo|Matsura-wan|松浦湾}} which is somewhat ambiguous.}}

=== Fudoki version (as Otohi) ===
In the alternate version of the old legend preserved in the {{illm|Hizen fudoki|ja|肥前国風土記|lt=''Hizen fudoki''}} (8th century), the Lady Otohi (or Otohihime)of {{nihongo|Shinuhara or Shinohara|篠原}}{{efn|The reading of the first character "篠" is commonly read "shino" in the modern day, but its pronunciation is given as {{nihongo|''shinu''|志弩}} in the ancient text.}} village appears as the name of the famed farewell-bidding wife of Ōtomo no Satehiko.<ref name="Cranston" /><ref name="nipponica-matsurasayohime" />{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|pp=11–12}}

"Otohi" may indeed be the woman's [[proper name]],{{Refn|{{nihongo|{{illm|Kamochi Masazumi|ja|鹿持雅澄}}'s ''Man'yōshū kogi''|万葉集古義}} (c. 1827), vol. 1, Lower Heaven (下天). And modern scholar [[Yūkichi Takeda]]'s position is similar.{{sfnp|Nagano|1974|pp=2–3}}}} however, it may also be a descriptor, with the word ''otohi'' having some meaning that is an extension of the ''oto'' ("youthful, innocent") element, or possibly meaning "those of younger age".{{efn|The word ''otohi'' as a common noun find usage in several ''kuni-bome'' or land-praising songs, and some scholars explain it to be a land-praising word.}}{{sfnp|Nagano|1974|pp=2–3}} The Lady Otohi is also referred to as ''otohime'' ("younger [sister] lady") in the poem inserted in the ''Fudoki'' account.{{efn|The English translator Cranston asserts that the similarity between Otohi and ''otohime'' is merely fortuitous.}}<ref name="Cranston"/>

This version recounts that the lady dropped a mirror given by her husband at a [[Ford (crossing)|ford]], or river crossing, and thenceforth the place was named {{nihongo|Kagami-no-watari|鏡の渡り|extra="Mirror Crossing"}}.<ref name="Cranston"/>{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|p=24}}{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=12}}

This version proceeds to tell the aftermath since the day the woman parts with Satehiko (waving her scarf at the Peak): she received visits from a look-alike of her husband for five nights in a row. Resolving to discover this man's true identity, she tied a "[[hemp]]" thread to the hem of his clothing and tracked him, thereby learning his true form: a snake residing at the marsh on the Scarf-Waving Peak. She was subsequently reported missing, and a search led to her skeletonized remains in the marsh.<ref name="Cranston" /><ref name="nipponica-matsurasayohime" />

A tale presenting this thread/yarn motif has been called the {{nihongo|clew-of-yarn type|苧環型説話|odamaki-gata setsuwa}} by Japanese scholars, and in the so-called {{nihongo|''Miwa-san setsuwa''|三輪山説話|extra="Mt. Miwa Story"}}, {{illm|Tamakushi hime|ja|玉櫛媛|lt=Ikutamayori-hime}} (Lady Ikutamayori) employs the same trick to discover the true form of the snake god [[Ōmononushi#Affair with Ikutamayorihime|Ōmononushi]].{{sfnp|Nagano|1974|pp=1, 5}}{{sfnp|Cranston|1998|pp=142–143, 74–75}}{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|The name "Mt. Miwa Story" is the name scholars use for this particular myth (Nagano; for discussion in English cf. Kelsey).<ref name=kelsey1981-miwasan-setsuwa/> But this title is ambiguous, because there is also a different tale involving another wife ({{illm|Yamato Todohimomosohime|ja|倭迹迹日百襲姫命}}) of the Mt. Miwa snake deity, which Cranston compares to the Matsura-hime legend. This wife was so shocked by the god's serpent form she took her own life in a brutal manner using a chopstick.<ref>{{harvp|Cranston|1998|p=142}}, referring to Tale 122 (Ōmononushi and Yamato Todohimomosohime no Mikoto) and commentary, [https://books.google.com/books?id=KqWjwalbmx4C&pg=PA75 p. 75].</ref> But the latter seems to be distinguished by calling it the {{nihongo|''Hashi-haka denshō/densetsu''|箸墓伝承/伝説|extra="legend of Hashihaka"}}, i.e. Hashihaka{{=}}'The Grave of the Chopstick[s]'.<ref name=suzuki_m/>}}

=== Suicidal drowning ===
{{illm|Fujiwara no Norikane|ja|藤原範兼}} wrote a commentary on [[waka poetry]] entitled {{nihongo|''Waka dōmōshō''|和歌童蒙抄}} (c. 1145) which probably due to a misreading of the ''Fudoki'', states that the lady, grasping the mirror given to her, sunk into the waters of the Kuri River (later called Kagami River, now {{illm|Matsura River|ja|松浦川}}<ref name=sagaken_no_chimei/>).{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|Norikane also misread or miscopied the lady's name as "{{nihongo|'''The Fourth Lady-child (noble daughter)'''|第四姫子}}".{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=17}}}}{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=17}}

=== Petrification version ===
[[File:Princess Sayo at Matsu-ura.PNG|thumb|''Matsura Sayo-hime''. Caption refers to the petrification{{efn|"..{{nihongo|the vital force (''[[qi|ki]]'') of her love, in its exact original shape, transformed into stone|恋慕の気凝りて、そのままに形(かたち)石となり}}".}}{{Right|{{Small|—by [[Utagawa Kuniyoshi]]. Series: ''Kenjo reppuden'' or "Stories of Wise and Strong Women"<!--賢女烈婦伝-->.}}}}]]

According to one version of this legend, she prayed with such fervour that she was transformed into stone.<ref name=pfoundes/> This petrification lore of Sayohime appears to be of later development, with its earliest attestation identified as [[renga]] poet {{illm|Bontōan|ja|梵灯庵}}'s ''Sodeshita shū'' (c. [[Ōei]] era, late 14th to early 15th century).<ref name=nakayama-apud-yoshioka/> This lore of Sayohime's petrification is thought to have developed from a misunderstanding: a misreading of {{illm|Jikkinshō|ja|十訓抄|lt=''Jikkinshō''}} (13th century), which ponders on the Sayohime legend and makes reference to the petrification motif taken from an old Chinese work called the {{illm|Youminglu|zh|幽明录|lt=''Youminglu''}}.{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=19}}<ref name=ishikawa/> Sayohime's petrification is also mentioned in ''Nihon meijo monogatari'' (1670).<ref>{{harvp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=24–25}}; p. 21 (English abstract), ''apud'' {{harvp|Satō|1966|p=37}}</ref>

[[image:佐用姫神社 - panoramio.jpg|thumb|left|Sayohime Shrine]]
Her supposed petrified remains, an example of a {{nihongo|''bōfuseki''|望夫石|extra="rock that contemplates the husband"}},{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=21–22}} is housed as the ''[[shintai]]'' ("body of the kami") at the Sayohime Shrine, an [[Setsumatsusha|undershrine]] of {{illm|Tashima Shrine|ja|田島神社|simple}} on {{illm|Kabe Island|ja|加部島}}.<ref name=joya/><ref name=japan-office1915/><ref name=murao/> The claim regarding her petrification on this island is given in a late account of the origin of this undershrine, preserved in the 19th century document called the {{nihongo|''Matsura komonjo''|松浦古文書}} (written during the [[Bunka]] era).<ref name=matsura-komonjo/> It states that the lady did not stop at the Scarf-Waving Peak bidding farewell, but she continued to a spot{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|At the place she saw the island, she called out Satehiko's name, hence the spot was named {{nihongo|''Yobu na no ura''|呼名の浦|extra=lit. the 'name-calling inlet-shore'}}, which later became the town of [[Yobuko, Saga|Yobuko]].<ref name=matsura-komonjo/><ref name=matsushiro/><ref name=nipponica-matsurasayohime/>}} from whose vantage point she beheld an island nearby. She then hopped on a fishing boat to that island, called the {{nihongo|Himekami-jima|姫神島}} island (present-day Kabe Island<ref name=yoshida/><ref name=yanagita-meii/>) where she climbed a "bit elevated spot" and there, out of sorrow, she turned intorock.<ref name=matsura-komonjo/> Commentators identify this elevatation as the {{nihongo|Tendō-dake|天童岳}} or {{nihongo|Dentō-dake|伝登岳}}.<ref name=yoshida/>

== Literary adaptations ==
During the early [[Muromachi Period]].<ref name=Noh/> The post-medieval narratives of {{nihongo|''Matsura Sayohime''|まつらさよひめ}} exists in several variant texts of various forms, e. g., hand-copied manuscripts and illustrated books of the ''Nara ehon'' type, sermons, scripts for the ''[[Jōruri (opera)|jōruri]]'' puppet theater, and printed books.<ref name=yokoyama/>

=== Noh play ===
'''''Matsura no Kagami''''' ('The Mirror of Matsura'), also referred to as ''Matsuura Sayohime'' [''sic.''] is also the title of a [[noh]] play about the character, whose authorship is ascribed to [[Zeami]].<ref name="Noh" />{{sfnp|Hare|1996|pp=46,55}}<ref name="SchechnerAppel1990" /> There has survived a holograph copy in Zeami's own hand entitled ''Matsura no Noh'', dated 10th month of [[1427]]/[[Ōei]] 24,<ref name=inoura&kawatake/><ref name=kuroda/> which closely matches the script text of ''Matsura no Kagami'' except for minor differences.<ref>[[Yamada Yoshio|Yamada, Yoshio]] (1928) "Kanze Sakon-shi zō Matsura no nō kaisetsu 観世左近氏蔵松浦之能解説" ({{harvp|Zeami|1928}}, Koten hozonkai edition).</ref>

The noh play, adopts the embellished legend where Sayohime drown herself in the sea while clutching the mirror, instead of just losing it.<ref name=Noh/>{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|p=24}} The suicidal drowning had been claimed in literature predating the noh play, such as the aforementioned 12th century ''Waka dōmōshō'' and Priest {{illm|Yua (priest)|ja|由阿|lt=Yua}}'s {{nihongo|''Shirin saiyōshō''|詞林采葉抄|extra="Commentary of Leaves from a Forest of Words", 1366}}.{{sfnp|Kanei|1977|p=306}}

=== Monogatari versions ===
There are some 7 texts of the fairy tale or ''monogatari''/''[[otogizōshi]]''  version of ''Sayohime''. The two full, or {{nihongo|"expanded versions"|広本|kōhon}} are the unillustrated "Akagi-bunko" library text entitled '''''Sayohime no sōshi'''''  dating to the  [[Keichō era]] (late 16th or early 17th century) and the illustrated book (''Nara ehon'') ''Sayohome'' in the possession of Kyoto University.{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|pp=261–262, 190}}{{sfnp|Sakaguchi|1982|p=161}}

The remaining ''monogatari'' texts belong to the {{nihongo|"abridged version"|略本|ryakubon}} group, and bear alternate titles such as {{nihongo|'''''Tsubosaka monogatari'''''|壺坂物語}}{{efn|Preserved as an ''emaki'' or illustrated scroll.}} or {{nihongo|'''''Chikubushima no honji'''''|ちくふしまのほんし|extra="The Chikubushima Deity in Her Original Form"{{Efn|An illustrated printed book is thus titled.}}}}.{{sfnp|Sakaguchi|1982|p=161}}{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|pp=261–263, 15}} The latter title (''~ [[Honji suijaku|honji]]'', the "original" Buddhist deity) is a reference to the tale purporting to reveal the origins of the principal goddess [[Benzaiten]] worshipped at [[Tsukubusuma Shrine|Chikubushima Shrine]] in [[Ōmi Province]], i.e., on an island in [[Lake Biwa]].{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|pp=12, 15}}

==== Plot Summary ====
The ''Sayohime no sōshi'' (A text) and the ''Sayohime''  (K text) have their opening setting relocated to Tsubosaka in [[Yamato Province]] (present-day [[Nara Prefecture]]),{{efn|The original legend is set in Matsuura in Kyushu, which is far south. In these ''monogatari'' versions, the girl's place of origin is Yamato Province (present-day Nara Prefecture), in central Japan. She later sells herself to a man residing up north in Mutsu.}} where a wealthy man named {{nihongo|Matsura Chōja|松浦長者|extra=A}} or {{nihongo|Lord Kyōgoku|京極殿|extra=K}} and his wife prayed to the [[Guanyin|Bodhisattva Kannon]] of [[Hase-dera]] until they were finally blessed with the birth of a daughter, Sayohime. But the rich man died penniless, and Sayohime could not afford to sponsor a memorial service for him except by selling herself. Her buyer was a man named {{nihongo|Konka no Tayū|こんかの大夫|extra=A}} (or "Gonga no Tayū"),{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|It is not actually clear whether "Konka" or "Gonga" or "Gonka" is the correct reading.{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|loc=p. 299 n6}}}} who unbeknownst to Sayohime intended to sacrifice her to the [[snake deity]] of his village in place of his own daughter. When presented to the snake, Sayohime read from the [[Lotus sutra]], enabling the deity to achieve enlightenment and shed its monstrous form. The deity then returned Sayohime to the care of her mother.<ref name=sekaidaihyakka-sayohimenososhi/>{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|pp=161–189}}

=== Buddhist perspective ===
The Kyoto text has been translated into English by R. Keller Kimbrough in ''Eight Buddhist Tales'' (2013).{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|p=261–262}} There is actually a {{illm|sekkyō-bushi|ja|説経節|lt=''sekkyō-bushi''}} (Buddhist "sermon ballad") version known as {{nihongo|''Matsura chōja''|まつら長じや|extra="The Millionaire of Matsura", 1661}}{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|Kimbrough mostly describes the text as a ''sekkyō-shōhon'' or "true text" of the ''sekkyō'' aka  ''sekkyō bushi'', but these various terms are explained in his glossary.{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|p=268}} Some researchers describe this title as a sermon puppet play (''sekkyō jōruri'').{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|p=21}}}}{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|The sermon text dated to [[Kanbun (era)|Kanbun]] 1 or 1661 is styled {{nihongo|''Matsura chōja''|まつら長じや}}, while the text dated to c. [[Hōei]] 1 (c. 1704) is styled {{nihongo|''Matsura chōja''|まつら長者}}.{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|p=262}}{{sfnp|Sakaguchi|1982|p=161}} Kimbrough calls these the 1661 and circa 1704 ''Matsura Chōja'' texts.}} which bears close correspondence to the Kyoto text of ''Sayohime'', and thought to derive from it.{{sfnp|Sakaguchi|1982|p=161}}{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|loc=p. 190, n42; p. 14, 262}}{{sfnp|Taguchi|1994|p=150}}

=== Self-sacrifice ===
The illustrations and text from the Frankfurt ''emaki'' manuscript has been analyzed and translated into German by Katja Triplett, with self-sacrifice and human sacrifice being the central themes.{{sfnp|Triplett|2004}} The heroine in an act of self-sacrifice {{nihongo|"sells oneself"|身売り|miuri}} into slavery, into the hands of a human trafficker in order to achieve a deed of filial piety.{{sfnp|Triplett|2004|pp=22, 223}} Needless to say, the common understanding (usual fate) of a ''miuri'' woman is that she is selling herself into prostitution, not slavery. But in the ''Sayohime'' saga we have [[Miracle|divine intervention]], and the {{illm|Kasuga no kami|ja|春日神|lt=Kasuga Myōjin}} deity of Nara disguised as an octagenerian priest intervenes so that Sayohime is discovered and bought by a seeker of a substitute virgin sacrifice (Gonga Tayū), rather than a brothel. Although the story is told as if Gonga  from [[Michinoku]] up north is the one obtaining sympathy and help from his clan deity (''ujigami'') based in Nara, the readers realize that the deity is actually responding to the pleas of the girl Sayohime from Nara.{{sfnp|Kimbrough|2013|pp=166–167}}{{sfnp|Triplett|2004|p=129}}

=== Puppet plays ===
The tales were also adapted to {{illm|oku jōruri|ja|奥浄瑠璃|lt=''oku jōruri''}}, a regional type of ''[[Jōruri (music)|jōruri]]'' puppet plays.{{efn|Under such titles as {{nihongo|''Chikubushima no honji''|竹生島之本地}} or {{nihongo|'''''Yakushinyorai honji Matsura Sayohime tanjōki'''''|薬師如来本地松浦佐夜姫誕生記}} .}}{{sfnp|Sakaguchi|1982|p=162}}

=== Gesaku ===
The petrification legend was adapted by  [[Takizawa Bakin]] (d. 1848) into a burlesque novel (''[[gesaku]]'') under the title {{nihongo|''Matsura Sayohime sekkonroku''|松浦佐用媛石魂録|extra="The Great Stone Spirit of Matsura"}}{{sfnp|Satō|1966|p=39}}<ref name=griffis/>

