{{afc comment|1=definitely notable[[User:Immanuelle|'''Immanuel'''le]] ❤️💚💙 [[User_talk:Immanuelle|(talk to the cutest Wikipedian)]] 06:21, 3 November 2023 (UTC) – draft}}
{{ill|Tashima Shrine|ja|田島神社|simple|6=draft|Tashima Shrine}} is a shrine situated on {{ill|Kabe Island|ja|加部島|ceb|Kabe_Shima_(pulo_sa_Hapon,_Saga-ken)|draft}} in [[Yobuko, Saga|Yobuko]] Town now, [[Karatsu, Saga|Karatsu]] City, [[Saga Prefecture]].<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QNw5D3Br8BoC&q=Tashima+Shrine |title=The Japan Magazine: A Representative Monthly of Things Japanese |date=1928 |publisher=Japan magazine Company |language=en|draft}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Hall |first=Jessica |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bmiYUKjEBMoC&q=Tashima+Shrine |title=The Deepest Edge |date=2003 |publisher=Signet |isbn=978-0-451-20796-8 |language=en|draft}}</ref> It is located in the area known as [[Matsurokoku]], which is believed to be the first land of the mainland of [[Wakoku]] as per [[Wajinden]] records. It is an important point for safe sea crossings to the continent, and has received significant orders from the central government since ancient times.

In earlier times, it was called 'Tajima Niimasu Kaminoyashiro'. It is the only [[Myojin Taisha]] in [[Hizen Province]], and was previously classified as [[Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Kokuhei Chusha]] due to renovations during the [[Meiji period]]. Currently, it is a [[beppyo shrine]] of the [[Association of Shinto Shrines]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-05-14 |title=別表神社とは？御朱印めぐりに参考になる「別表神社一覧」とマップ {{!}} 開運戦隊ゴシュインジャー |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230514023648/https://jinja-gosyuin.com/beppyou/ |access-date=2023-12-02 |website=web.archive.org}}</ref> It is associated with [[Matsura Sayohime]] who is said to be buried on the site<ref name="joya" /><ref name="japan-office1915" /><ref name="murao" />. It is a Munakata shrine<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kalland |first=Arne |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wp4xhRXsARcC&dq=Tashima+Shrine&pg=PA47 |title=Fishing Villages in Tokugawa, Japan |date=1995-01-01 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-1632-2 |language=en|draft}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Rots |first1=Aike P. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pcjZDwAAQBAJ&dq=Tashima+Shrine&pg=PT104 |title=Sacred Heritage in Japan |last2=Teeuwen |first2=Mark |date=2020-04-02 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-000-04563-5 |language=en|draft}}</ref> and is said to be the original shrine (roots) of [[Munakata Taisha]], so it is sometimes called Moto-Munakata
[[File:Wajinden_diagram.svg|thumb|map illustrating the path from the [[Daifang Commandery|Daifeng commandery]] to [[Yamatai]], and its distances in the [[Wajinden]]. See Matsurokoku around the middle for the locatiton of the shrine]]

== Mythology ==
[[File:Princess Sayo at Matsu-ura.PNG|thumb|''Matsura Sayo-hime''. Caption refers to the petrification{{efn|"..{{nihongo|the vital force (''[[qi|ki]]'') of her love, in its exact original shape, transformed into stone|恋慕の気凝りて、そのままに形(かたち)石となり}}".}}{{Right|{{Small|—by [[Utagawa Kuniyoshi]]. Series: ''Kenjo reppuden'' or "Stories of Wise and Strong Women"<!--賢女烈婦伝-->.}}}}]]

[[image:佐用姫神社 - panoramio.jpg|thumb|left|Sayohime Shrine]]According to a version of the legend of [[Matsura Sayohime]], she prayed with such fervour that she was transformed into stone.<ref name="pfoundes" /> This petrification lore of Sayohime appears to be of later development, with its earliest attestation identified as [[renga]] poet {{illm|Bontōan|ja|梵灯庵|draft}}'s ''Sodeshita shū'' (c. [[Ōei]] era, late 14th to early 15th century).<ref name="nakayama-apud-yoshioka" /> This lore of Sayohime's petrification is thought to have developed from a misunderstanding: a misreading of {{illm|Jikkinshō|ja|十訓抄|draft|lt=''Jikkinshō''}} (13th century), which ponders on the Sayohime legend and makes reference to the petrification motif taken from an old Chinese work called the {{illm|You Ming Lu|zh|幽明录|draft|lt=''Youminglu''}}.{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=19}}<ref name="ishikawa" /> Sayohime's petrification is also mentioned in ''Nihon meijo monogatari'' (1670).<ref>{{harvp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=24–25}}; p. 21 (English abstract), ''apud'' {{harvp|Satō|1966|p=37}}</ref>

Her supposed petrified remains, an example of a {{nihongo|''bōfuseki''|望夫石|extra="rock that contemplates the husband"}},{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=21–22}} is housed as the ''[[shintai]]'' ("body of the kami") at the Sayohime Shrine, an [[Setsumatsusha|undershrine]] of {{illm|Tashima Shrine|ja|田島神社|simple}} on {{ill|Kabe Island|ja|加部島|ceb|Kabe_Shima_(pulo_sa_Hapon,_Saga-ken)|draft}}.<ref name="joya" /><ref name="japan-office1915" /><ref name="murao" /> The claim regarding her petrification on this island is given in a late account of the origin of this undershrine, preserved in the 19th century document called the {{nihongo|''Matsura komonjo''|松浦古文書}} (written during the [[Bunka]] era).<ref name="matsura-komonjo" /> It states that the lady did not stop at the Scarf-Waving Peak bidding farewell, but she continued to a spot{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|At the place she saw the island, she called out Satehiko's name, hence the spot was named {{nihongo|''Yobu na no ura''|呼名の浦|extra=lit. the 'name-calling inlet-shore'}}, which later became the town of [[Yobuko, Saga|Yobuko]].<ref name=matsura-komonjo/><ref name=matsushiro/><ref name=nipponica-matsurasayohime/>}} from whose vantage point she beheld an island nearby. She then hopped on a fishing boat to that island, called the {{nihongo|Himekami-jima|姫神島}} island (present-day Kabe Island<ref name="yoshida" /><ref name="yanagita-meii" />) where she climbed a "bit elevated spot" and there, out of sorrow, she turned intorock.<ref name="matsura-komonjo" /> Commentators identify this elevatation as the {{nihongo|Tendō-dake|天童岳}} or {{nihongo|Dentō-dake|伝登岳}}.<ref name="yoshida" />

