== Mythology ==
[[File:Princess Sayo at Matsu-ura.PNG|thumb|''Matsura Sayo-hime''. Caption refers to the petrification{{efn|"..{{nihongo|the vital force (''[[qi|ki]]'') of her love, in its exact original shape, transformed into stone|恋慕の気凝りて、そのままに形(かたち)石となり}}".}}{{Right|{{Small|—by [[Utagawa Kuniyoshi]]. Series: ''Kenjo reppuden'' or "Stories of Wise and Strong Women"<!--賢女烈婦伝-->.}}}}]]

[[image:佐用姫神社 - panoramio.jpg|thumb|left|Sayohime Shrine]]According to a version of the legend of [[Matsura Sayohime]], she prayed with such fervour that she was transformed into stone.<ref name="pfoundes-1878a">{{cite journal |last=Pfoundes |first=C |author-link=<!--Charles James William Pfoundes (1840–1907)--> |year=1878 |title=The Loving Wife |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ahd5yMw3Or8C&pg=PA131 |journal=The Folk-Lore Record |volume=1 |page=131 |jstor=1252349}}; {{cite book |last=Pfoundes |first=C |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TiINAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA178 |title=Fu-so Mimo Bukuro: A Budget of Japanese Notes |publisher=Japan Mai |year=1875 |location=Yokohama |page=178 |author-mask=2 |author-link=<!--Charles James William Pfoundes (1840–1907)-->}}</ref> This petrification lore of Sayohime appears to be of later development, with its earliest attestation identified as [[renga]] poet {{illm|Bontōan|ja|梵灯庵|draft}}'s ''Sodeshita shū'' (c. [[Ōei]] era, late 14th to early 15th century).<ref name="nakayama-1943a" /> This lore of Sayohime's petrification is thought to have developed from a misunderstanding: a misreading of {{illm|Jikkinshō|ja|十訓抄|draft|lt=''Jikkinshō''}} (13th century), which ponders on the Sayohime legend and makes reference to the petrification motif taken from an old Chinese work called the {{illm|You Ming Lu|zh|幽明录|draft|lt=''Youminglu''}}.{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=19}}<ref name="ishikawa-1892a" /> Sayohime's petrification is also mentioned in ''Nihon meijo monogatari'' (1670).<ref>{{harvp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=24–25}}; p. 21 (English abstract), ''apud'' {{harvp|Satō|1966|p=37}}</ref>

Her supposed petrified remains, an example of a {{nihongo|''bōfuseki''|望夫石|extra="rock that contemplates the husband"}},{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=21–22}} is housed as the ''[[shintai]]'' ("body of the kami") at the Sayohime Shrine, an [[Setsumatsusha|undershrine]] of {{illm|Tashima Shrine|ja|田島神社|simple}} on {{ill|Kabe Island|ja|加部島|ceb|Kabe_Shima_(pulo_sa_Hapon,_Saga-ken)|draft}}.<ref name="joya-1963a" /><ref name="the-japan-office-editors-1915a" /><ref name="murao-1968a" /> The claim regarding her petrification on this island is given in a late account of the origin of this undershrine, preserved in the 19th century document called the {{nihongo|''Matsura komonjo''|松浦古文書}} (written during the [[Bunka]] era).<ref name="matsura-komonjo-松浦古文書-volume-1-1934a" /> It states that the lady did not stop at the Scarf-Waving Peak bidding farewell, but she continued to a spot{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|At the place she saw the island, she called out Satehiko's name, hence the spot was named {{nihongo|''Yobu na no ura''|呼名の浦|extra=lit. the 'name-calling inlet-shore'}}, which later became the town of [[Yobuko, Saga|Yobuko]].<ref name="matsura-komonjo-松浦古文書-volume-1-1934a"/><ref name="kyukeisha-1925a"/><ref name="shogakukan-1994a"/>}} from whose vantage point she beheld an island nearby. She then hopped on a fishing boat to that island, called the {{nihongo|Himekami-jima|姫神島}} island (present-day Kabe Island<ref name="yoshida-1992a">{{citation |last=Yoshida |first=Shūsaku |title=Denshō no Tsukushi-otome: Matsura Sayohime denshō |date=1992 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pgAZAQAAMAAJ&q=姫神島 |journal=Fukuoka Jogakuin University bulletin<!--福岡女学院大学紀要--> |volume=2 |page=77<!--63–--> |script-title=ja:伝承の〈筑紫をとめ〉-松浦佐用姫伝承- |author-link=:ja:吉田修作}}; {{citation |last=Yoshida |first=Shūsaku |title=Bungei denshōron: denshō no wotoko to wotome |date=1998 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2lE0AQAAIAAJ&q=姫神島 |page=242 |script-title=ja:文芸伝承論: 伝承の「をとこ」と「をとめ」 |location= |publisher=Ōfū<!--おうふう--> |isbn=<!--4273030381, -->9784273030384 |author-link=:ja:吉田修作}}</ref><ref name="yanagita-1971a">{{citation |last=Yanagita |first=Kunio |title=Nihon densetsu meii |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2qvSAAAAMAAJ&q=%22呼名の浦%22 |page=189 |year=1971 |script-title=ja:日本伝説名彙 |location= |publisher=Nihon hōsō shuppan kyōkai<!--日本放送出版協会--> |authorlink=Kunio Yanagita |origyear=1950}}</ref>) where she climbed a "bit elevated spot" and there, out of sorrow, she turned intorock.<ref name="matsura-komonjo-松浦古文書-volume-1-1934a" /> Commentators identify this elevatation as the {{nihongo|Tendō-dake|天童岳}} or {{nihongo|Dentō-dake|伝登岳}}.<ref name="yoshida-1992a" />

