{{expand Japanese|田島神社|draft}}[[ja:田島神社]]
{{ill|Tashima Shrine|ja|田島神社|simple|6=draft|Tashima Shrine}} is a shrine situated on {{ill|Kabe Island|ja|加部島|ceb|Kabe_Shima_(pulo_sa_Hapon,_Saga-ken)|draft}} in [[Yobuko, Saga|Yobuko]] Town now, [[Karatsu, Saga|Karatsu]] City, [[Saga Prefecture]].<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QNw5D3Br8BoC&q=Tashima+Shrine |title=The Japan Magazine: A Representative Monthly of Things Japanese |date=1928 |publisher=Japan magazine Company |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Hall |first=Jessica |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bmiYUKjEBMoC&q=Tashima+Shrine |title=The Deepest Edge |date=2003 |publisher=Signet |isbn=978-0-451-20796-8 |language=en}}</ref> It is located in the area known as [[Matsurokoku]], which is believed to be the first land of the mainland of [[Wakoku]] as per [[Wajinden]] records. It is an important point for safe sea crossings to the continent, and has received significant orders from the central government since ancient times.

In earlier times, it was called 'Tajima Niimasu Kaminoyashiro'. It is the only [[Myojin Taisha]] in [[Hizen Province]], and was previously classified as [[Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Kokuhei Chusha]] due to renovations during the [[Meiji period]]. Currently, it is a [[beppyo shrine]] of the [[Association of Shinto Shrines]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-05-14 |title=別表神社とは？御朱印めぐりに参考になる「別表神社一覧」とマップ {{!}} 開運戦隊ゴシュインジャー |url=https://jinja-gosyuin.com/beppyou/ |access-date=2023-12-02 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230514023648/https://jinja-gosyuin.com/beppyou/ |archive-date=2023-05-14 }}</ref> It is associated with [[Matsura Sayohime]] who is said to be buried on the site<ref name="joya-1963a">{{cite book |last=Jōya |first=Moku |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=daczAQAAIAAJ&q=%22Sayohime+shrine%22 |title=Mock Jōya's Things Japanese |date=1963 |publisher=Tokyo News Service Press |page=222 |authorlink=<!--城谷 黙 (1884-1963) -->}}</ref><ref name="the-japan-office-editors-1915a">{{citation |author=The Japan Office editors<!--no byline--> |title=September Sights: The Season of Festivals |date=1915 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=h4o0AAAAIAAJ&q=+shrines+Tajima |journal=Japan |number=59 |page=16<!--11–20--> |others=(based on material supplied by) [[Shiga Shigetaka]] |publisher=The Japan Office}}</ref><ref name="murao-1968a">{{citation |last=Murao |first=Rikitarō |title=Tsukishi no no to Chikuhi no umi no kaiko: Nihon&kaigai shūkyō kōshō kenkyū |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fOZCAQAAIAAJ&q=望夫石 |journal=Wasada shōgaku<!--早稲田商学/早稻田商學--> |issue=205 |page=103<!--93–--> |year=1968 |script-title=ja:「筑紫の野」と「筑肥の海」の懐古―日本・海外宗教交渉略史研究― |author-link=<!--村尾力太郎-->}}</ref>. It is a Munakata shrine<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kalland |first=Arne |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wp4xhRXsARcC&dq=Tashima+Shrine&pg=PA47 |title=Fishing Villages in Tokugawa, Japan |date=1995-01-01 |publisher=University of Hawaii Press |isbn=978-0-8248-1632-2 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Rots |first1=Aike P. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pcjZDwAAQBAJ&dq=Tashima+Shrine&pg=PT104 |title=Sacred Heritage in Japan |last2=Teeuwen |first2=Mark |date=2020-04-02 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-000-04563-5 |language=en}}</ref> and is said to be the original shrine (roots) of [[Munakata Taisha]], so it is sometimes called Moto-Munakata
[[File:Wajinden_diagram.svg|thumb|map illustrating the path from the [[Daifang Commandery|Daifeng commandery]] to [[Yamatai]], and its distances in the [[Wajinden]]. See [[Matsurokoku]] around the middle for the locatiton of the shrine]]

== Mythology ==
[[File:Princess Sayo at Matsu-ura.PNG|thumb|''Matsura Sayo-hime''. Caption refers to the petrification{{efn|"..{{nihongo|the vital force (''[[qi|ki]]'') of her love, in its exact original shape, transformed into stone|恋慕の気凝りて、そのままに形(かたち)石となり}}".}}{{Right|{{Small|—by [[Utagawa Kuniyoshi]]. Series: ''Kenjo reppuden'' or "Stories of Wise and Strong Women"<!--賢女烈婦伝-->.}}}}]]

[[image:佐用姫神社 - panoramio.jpg|thumb|left|Sayohime Shrine]]According to a version of the legend of [[Matsura Sayohime]], she prayed with such fervour that she was transformed into stone.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Pfoundes |first=C |author-link=<!--Charles James William Pfoundes (1840–1907)--> |year=1878 |title=The Loving Wife |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ahd5yMw3Or8C&pg=PA131 |journal=The Folk-Lore Record |volume=1 |page=131 |jstor=1252349}}; {{cite book |last=Pfoundes |first=C |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TiINAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA178 |title=Fu-so Mimo Bukuro: A Budget of Japanese Notes |publisher=Japan Mai |year=1875 |location=Yokohama |page=178 |author-mask=2 |author-link=<!--Charles James William Pfoundes (1840–1907)-->}}</ref> This petrification lore of Sayohime appears to be of later development, with its earliest attestation identified as [[renga]] poet {{illm|Bontōan|ja|梵灯庵}}'s ''Sodeshita shū'' (c. [[Ōei]] era, late 14th to early 15th century).<ref>{{citation |last=Nakayama |first=Tarō |title=Shinkō to minzoku |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=t0ZfpZvZD9sC&q=佐用姫化石 |page=211 |year=1943 |script-title=ja:信仰と民俗 |chapter=Bōfuseki |chapter-url=https://dl.ndl.go.jp/info:ndljp/pid/1040027/111 |publisher=Mikasa Shobo<!--三笠書房--> |authorlink=:ja:中山太郎 (民俗学者) |script-chapter=ja:望夫石}} ''apud'' [[:ja:吉岡郷甫|Yoshioka, Kyōsuke]] (1906), "''Matsura Sayohime no densetsu''<!--松浦佐用姫の伝説-->", ''Teikoku bungaku''<!--帝國文學--> '''12''' (7).</ref> This lore of Sayohime's petrification is thought to have developed from a misunderstanding: a misreading of {{illm|Jikkinshō|ja|十訓抄|draft|lt=''Jikkinshō''}} (13th century), which ponders on the Sayohime legend and makes reference to the petrification motif taken from an old Chinese work called the {{illm|You Ming Lu|zh|幽明录|draft|lt=''Youminglu''}}.{{sfnp|Yabu|2006|p=19}}<ref>{{citation |last=Ishikawa |first=Masamochi |title=Nezame no susabi 2 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=NANJAAAAMAAJ&pg=PP652 |work=Hyakka setsurin |volume=3 |page=642 |year=1892 |script-title=ja:ねざめのすさび 2 |publisher=Yoshikawa kobunkan<!--吉川弘文館--> |authorlink=Ishikawa Masamochi |script-work=ja:百家説林 |origyear=1805}}</ref> Sayohime's petrification is also mentioned in ''Nihon meijo monogatari'' (1670).<ref>{{harvp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=24–25}}; p. 21 (English abstract), ''apud'' {{harvp|Satō|1966|p=37}}</ref>

Her supposed petrified remains, an example of a {{nihongo|''bōfuseki''|望夫石|extra="rock that contemplates the husband"}},{{sfnp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=21–22}} is housed as the ''[[shintai]]'' ("body of the kami") at the Sayohime Shrine, an [[Setsumatsusha|undershrine]] of {{illm|Tashima Shrine|ja|田島神社|simple}} on {{ill|Kabe Island|ja|加部島|ceb|Kabe_Shima_(pulo_sa_Hapon,_Saga-ken)|draft}}.<ref name="joya-1963a" /><ref name="the-japan-office-editors-1915a" /><ref name="murao-1968a" /> The claim regarding her petrification on this island is given in a late account of the origin of this undershrine, preserved in the 19th century document called the {{nihongo|''Matsura komonjo''|松浦古文書}} (written during the [[Bunka]] era).<ref name="matsura-komonjo-松浦古文書-volume-1-1934a" /> It states that the lady did not stop at the Scarf-Waving Peak bidding farewell, but she continued to a spot{{Refn|group="lower-alpha"|At the place she saw the island, she called out Satehiko's name, hence the spot was named {{nihongo|''Yobu na no ura''|呼名の浦|extra=lit. the 'name-calling inlet-shore'}}, which later became the town of [[Yobuko, Saga|Yobuko]].<ref name="matsura-komonjo-松浦古文書-volume-1-1934a">''Matsura komonjo'' 松浦古文書 Volume 1('jō'), "Ch. 5: ''Sayohime jinja no koto'' 佐用姫神社之事", in: {{citation|editor-last=Yoshimura |editor-first=Shigesaburo |editor-link=<!--吉村茂三郎--> |title=Matsuura zōsho |script-title=ja:「松浦叢書 |volume=1 |location=Karatsu |publisher=<!--吉村茂三郎--> |year=1934 |url=https://dl.ndl.go.jp/info:ndljp/pid/1214367/72 |pages=81–82}}.<!--cited by {{harvp|Kim Kyonran|1998|pp=}}, n9--></ref><ref>{{citation|editor-last=Matsushiro |editor-first=Matsutarō |editor-link=<!--松代松太郎--> |title=Higashi-Matsuura-gun shi |script-title=ja:東松浦郡史 |location=|publisher=Kyūkei-sha<!--久敬社--> |year=1925 |url=https://dl.ndl.go.jp/info:ndljp/pid/1020689/295 |page=559}}</ref><ref>{{cite encyclopedia|editor= |editor-link= |title=Matsura-sayohime |script-title=ja:松浦佐用姫 まつらさよひめ |encyclopedia=Nihon daihyakka jiten (Nipponica)<!--日本大百科全書(ニッポニカ)--> |location= |publisher=Shogakukan |year=1994|url=https://kotobank.jp/word/%E6%9D%BE%E6%B5%A6%E4%BD%90%E7%94%A8%E5%A7%AB-136794 |page= |isbn=}}</ref>}} from whose vantage point she beheld an island nearby. She then hopped on a fishing boat to that island, called the {{nihongo|Himekami-jima|姫神島}} island (present-day Kabe Island<ref name="yoshida-1992a">{{citation |last=Yoshida |first=Shūsaku |title=Denshō no Tsukushi-otome: Matsura Sayohime denshō |date=1992 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=pgAZAQAAMAAJ&q=姫神島 |journal=Fukuoka Jogakuin University bulletin<!--福岡女学院大学紀要--> |volume=2 |page=77<!--63–--> |script-title=ja:伝承の〈筑紫をとめ〉-松浦佐用姫伝承- |author-link=:ja:吉田修作}}; {{citation |last=Yoshida |first=Shūsaku |title=Bungei denshōron: denshō no wotoko to wotome |date=1998 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2lE0AQAAIAAJ&q=姫神島 |page=242 |script-title=ja:文芸伝承論: 伝承の「をとこ」と「をとめ」 |location= |publisher=Ōfū<!--おうふう--> |isbn=<!--4273030381, -->9784273030384 |author-link=:ja:吉田修作}}</ref><ref>{{citation |last=Yanagita |first=Kunio |title=Nihon densetsu meii |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2qvSAAAAMAAJ&q=%22呼名の浦%22 |page=189 |year=1971 |script-title=ja:日本伝説名彙 |location= |publisher=Nihon hōsō shuppan kyōkai<!--日本放送出版協会--> |authorlink=Kunio Yanagita |origyear=1950}}</ref>) where she climbed a "bit elevated spot" and there, out of sorrow, she turned intorock.<ref name="matsura-komonjo-松浦古文書-volume-1-1934a" /> Commentators identify this elevatation as the {{nihongo|Tendō-dake|天童岳}} or {{nihongo|Dentō-dake|伝登岳}}.<ref name="yoshida-1992a" />

